-- title:
--   Degree-one η-sections with tangent ratio -y(η)
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime, $k$ an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$, and $\iota : W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2}) \to W(k)$ a ring homomorphism (`Zp2 p` is $W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})$). Write $O = W(k)$, $\bar O = O/pO$ (the ideal `pIdeal p O` being $(p)$), and let $\bar\iota$ denote the composite of $\iota$ with $O \to \bar O$. Let $E =$ `EdgeFamily.edgeRingCharP p` $\bar O$ be the edge chart ring over $\bar O$, an $\bar O$-algebra with its two distinguished elements $\xi =$ `EdgeFamily.edgeRingCharP.ξ` and $\eta =$ `EdgeFamily.edgeRingCharP.η`, and let $j_E$ be the structure map $\iota$ followed by $O \to \bar O \to E$.
--
--   Data and hypotheses on the formal $O_D$-module $\Phi$ over $\bar O$: $\Phi$ is special for $\bar\iota$ (`hΦ`: its zero and one Lie pieces are complementary and both invertible), has height $4$ (`hΦ4`: the kernel of the action of $p$ has degree $p^4$), its zero Lie piece is annihilated by `lieVarpi` (`h0Φ`), and the graded pieces $0$ and $1$ of its Cartier module for $\bar\iota$ are complementary additive subgroups (`hcΦ`), so that the graded Cartier module datum $D_\Phi = \Phi$`.toGradedCartierModuleData` $\bar\iota$ `hcΦ` is available. Further, $r_\Phi$ is an additive map $\mathbb{Z}_p^2 \to D_\Phi.\mathrm{NMod}$ which, by `hrΦ`, maps the whole of $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$ bijectively onto the degree-$0$ $\eta$-piece $D_\Phi$`.etaPiece L _ 0` (the intersection of $D_\Phi.\mathrm{eta}$ with the degree-$0$ $N$-piece) for every canonical $L$-map $L$ on $D_\Phi$.
--
--   An element $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ is given with $g = \mathrm{diag}(p,1)$ (`hg`).
--
--   Edge-family data: $X$ is a formal $O_D$-module over $E$ and $\gamma_0,\gamma_1$ elements of its Cartier module such that $\gamma$ is a homogeneous $V$-basis for $j_E$ (`hγ`: $\gamma_i$ lies in the graded piece $i$ and the matrix of tangents $(\mathrm{tangent}(\gamma_i)_l)$ has unit determinant), the structure constants of $X$ on $\gamma$ are the edge constants `EdgeFamily.edgeRingConstants` formed from $\xi$ and $\eta$ (`hγa`), $X$ is special for $j_E$ (`hXs`) and has height $4$ (`hX4`).
--
--   The node point: $f_0 : E \to \bar O$ is a ring homomorphism splitting the structure map $\bar O \to E$ (`hf₀`) with $f_0(\xi) = 0$ (`hf₀ξ`) and $f_0(\eta) = 0$ (`hf₀η`). Next, $m$ is a natural number and $\rho_0$ a $2$-tuple of power series over $\bar O$ which is an isogeny $\Phi \to f_{0*}X$ of height $4m$ (`hρ₀`). Let $t_0$ be the rigidified object over $\bar O$ with underlying module $f_{0*}X$, level $m$ and $\rho$ the reduction of $\rho_0$ modulo $p$. The hypotheses `hOD₀`, `hcb`, `hcΦg` require, respectively, that the reduction $t_0.\rho$ be a homomorphism of formal $O_D$-modules from $t_0$`.Φbar` to $t_0$`.Xbar`, and that the degree-$0$ and degree-$1$ graded pieces be complementary for $t_0$`.XbarS` and for `PhibarS`, both taken along the identity of $\bar O$. Finally, for an exponent $a$, the normalisation hypothesis `hN` states that for each $i \in \{0,1\}$,
--   $$p^a \cdot \mathrm{rigidNum}(\delta_i) = p^{a+m} \cdot w_i,$$
--   where $\mathrm{rigidNum} = t_0$`.rigidNum ι hcΦ rΦ` (the composite of $r_\Phi$ with the $N$-module maps induced by base change $\Phi \to$ `PhibarS` and by $t_0.\rho$), $\delta_i =$ `Pi.single i 1` is the $i$-th standard vector of $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$, and, writing $\bar\gamma_i$ for the successive base changes of $\gamma_i$ along $f_0$, along $\bar O \to \bar O/p\bar O$ and along `reduceMap` of the identity, $w_0 = \mathrm{nMk}(\bar\gamma_0,0)$ and $w_1 = \mathrm{nVarpi}(\mathrm{nMk}(\bar\gamma_1,0))$ in the $N$-module of $t_0$`.XbarS`.
--
--   The node isogeny: $\rho_1$ is a homomorphism of formal $O_D$-modules over $E$ from the base change of $f_{0*}X$ to $E$ towards $X$, whose induced map on Cartier modules sends the base change of $\gamma_0$ to $p\,\gamma_0 - V([\eta^{p^2}]\,\gamma_1)$ (`hρ₁0`) and the base change of $\gamma_1$ to $p\,\gamma_1 - V([\xi^{p^2}]\,\gamma_0)$ (`hρ₁1`), where $[\,\cdot\,]$ is the Teichmüller lift and $V$ is `verschiebungInt`; and $\rho_1$ is an isogeny of height $4$ (`hρ₁h`).
--
--   The geometric point: $\Omega$ is an algebraically closed field which is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra and $y : E \to \Omega$ a ring homomorphism with $y(\xi) = 0$ (`hyξ`) and $y(\eta) \neq 0$ (`hyη`).
--
--   Let $t$ be the rigidified object over $E$ with underlying module $X$, level $m+1$ and $\rho$ the composite over $E/pE$ of the reduction of $\rho_0$ (pushed along $\bar O \to E \to E/pE$) followed by the reduction of the series of $\rho_1$, and let $t_y = t$`.map y` be its base change along $y$; let $j_y$ denote $\iota$ followed by $O \to \bar O \to E \xrightarrow{y} \Omega$, and let `Rigidified.awayHom (1 : Ω)` be the localisation map of $\Omega$ away from $1$.
--
--   The conclusion asserts the existence of the following data. First, proofs `hOD` that $t_y.\rho$ is a homomorphism of formal $O_D$-modules from $t_y$`.Φbar` to $t_y$`.Xbar`, and `hc`, `hcb`, `hcΦg` that the degree-$0$ and degree-$1$ graded pieces are complementary for $t_y$`.XS`, for $t_y$`.XbarS` and for `PhibarS`, all taken along `awayHom (1 : Ω)` and $j_y$. Write $D$ for the resulting graded Cartier module datum $t_y$`.XS (awayHom 1)`$.$`toGradedCartierModuleData (jS ι _ (awayHom 1)) hc`, with underlying Cartier module $M = D.M$. Secondly, an additive map $L : M \to D.\mathrm{NMod}$ which is a canonical $L$-map (`hL`). Thirdly, two elements $z_0, z_1 \in D.\mathrm{NMod}$ which are $\eta$-sections in degree $i = 1$ for the vectors
--   $$v_0 = p^{-1} e_1, \qquad v_1 = e_0$$
--   of $\mathbb{Q}_p^2$, where $e_0, e_1$ are the standard basis vectors of the standard full lattice $\mathbb{Z}_p^2 \subset \mathbb{Q}_p^2$; being an $\eta$-section in degree $1$ for a vector $v$ means that the element lies in $D$`.etaPiece L _ 1`, the intersection of $D.\mathrm{eta}$ with the degree-$1$ $N$-piece, and that the relation `LatticeRel` holds for the reduced datum $t_y$`.XbarS`, the level $m+1$, the rigidification numerator $t_y$`.rigidNum ι hcΦ rΦ`, the element $t_y$`.etaRed` applied to $\mathrm{nVarpi}(z)$, and the vector $p\,v$. Fourthly, two elements $m_0, m_1 \in M$ such that:
--
--   (i) the class of $m_0$ in $M / V M$ (the quotient by $D.\mathrm{vRange}$, the image of the Verschiebung) equals $D.u\,L$ evaluated at $z_0$, taken as an element of $D.\mathrm{eta}$;
--
--   (ii) likewise the class of $m_1$ equals $D.u\,L$ evaluated at $z_1$;
--
--   (iii) for every $j \in \{0,1\}$, $\mathrm{tangent}(m_1)_j = -\,c'\cdot \mathrm{tangent}(m_0)_j$, where $c'$ is the image of $y(\eta)$ under `awayHom (1 : Ω)`;
--
--   (iv) there exists $j \in \{0,1\}$ with $\mathrm{tangent}(m_0)_j \neq 0$.
--
--   Within the Čerednik–Drinfeld part of the development, this is the computation of the two degree-one $\eta$-sections of the rigidified edge family at a geometric point of the $\eta$-branch ($y(\xi)=0$, $y(\eta)\neq 0$), together with the ratio $-y(\eta)$ between the tangents of their chosen representatives in the Cartier module modulo the Verschiebung. It is used in the identification of the kernel at such a point as an explicit span, in [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.ker_eq_span_of_lattice_eq_of_isCartierQuadruple_map_edge_of_apply_xi_eq_zero_of_apply_eta_ne_zero`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.ker_eq_span_of_lattice_eq_of_isCartierQuadruple_map_edge_of_apply_xi_eq_zero_of_apply_eta_ne_zero), where the sections correspond to the basis $e_0, p^{-1}e_1$ of the lattice attached to the vertex $\mathrm{diag}(p,1)$.
